<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Priscillian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 385</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Priscillian was a Christian ascetic teacher in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> in the late 4th century. His movement, which stressed strict asceticism and the reading of apocryphal texts, was accused of heresy and sorcery. He was tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and executed in 385 on the orders of the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a>, the first Christian put to death for heresy, and his followers remained strong in Gallaecia for two centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Priscillian was born around 340 into the nobility, possibly in Gallaecia in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, and was well educated. About 370 he began a movement in favour of asceticism. He urged the study of apocryphal books as well as of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, which he valued as helpful in telling truth from error rather than as inspired. His eloquence and severely ascetic example won over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, among them Instantius and Salvianus. He taught that the perfect in body, mind and spirit were celibate or, if married, continent, while holding out hope of pardon to those who could not reach full perfection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> opposed to him, notably Hyginus of Corduba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a> of Augusta Emerita, accused his teachings of being gnostic. A synod at Caesaraugusta in 380 condemned practices such as eating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> at home instead of in church, women praying alongside men, fasting on Sundays and withdrawing to the mountains during Lent; it did not name Priscillian. He was then elected bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> and consecrated by Instantius and Salvianus. His rival Ithacius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossonoba">Ossonoba</a> obtained an edict from the emperor Gratian against false bishops and Manichees, and Priscillian went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to plead his case, but neither Pope Damasus I nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a> received him. Through the magister officiorum Macedonius the edict was withdrawn, and he returned to his see.</p><p>In 383 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnus-maximus">Magnus Maximus</a> rebelled against Gratian, who was assassinated, and Maximus made Augusta Treverorum his residence. There Ithacius brought his case. Priscillian was tried by a secular court on charges that included sorcery, a capital offence, was made to confess to obscene doctrines and nocturnal meetings, and was executed by the sword around 385 with five companions. Maximus is also said to have wished to enrich his treasury from the confiscated property of the condemned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The execution is seen as the first case of secular justice intervening in a church matter and the first killing of a Christian by other Christians for heresy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-siricius">Pope Siricius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Martin of Tours</a> protested, largely on the ground that a civil court should not decide an ecclesiastical case. Priscillian was honoured as a martyr, especially in Gallaecia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscillianism">Priscillianism</a> lasted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> until the second half of the 6th century; the second synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bracara-augusta">Bracara Augusta</a> condemned it again in 563.</p><p>His writings were thought lost until Georg Schepss found eleven tracts at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> in 1885; four of them describing the trial appear to be by a close follower. It is hard to separate what Priscillian himself taught from what his enemies, and later groups called Priscillianist, ascribed to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Priscillian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity spread, and the ascetic movement of <b>Priscillian</b>, executed in 385, found strong support here.</blockquote><a href="/state/gallaecia">Gallaecia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.21,-4.35&amp;year=375" aria-label="Hispania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Priscillian"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4716 4126h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4716 4126h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.21,-4.35&amp;year=375">What was here in 375 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
